Analysts make what is known as a convexity adjustment to account for the total difference between the two rates. One popular adjustment is
Forward rate = Futures rate - \frac{1}{2}\sigma^{2}T_{1}T_{2}
where, as above, T_{1} is the time to maturity of the futures contract and T_{2} is the time to the maturity of the rate underlying the futures contract. The variable ! is the standard deviation of the change in the short-term interest rate in 1 year. Both rates are expressed with continuous compounding.

