Definition: Another possible policy is for the firm to pay a dividend of $110 per share on the first date (Date 1), which is, of course, a total dividend of $11,000. Because the cash flow is only $10,000, an extra $1,000 must somehow be raised. One way to do this is to issue $1,000 worth of bonds or stock at Date 1. Assume that stock is issued. The new stockholders will desire enough cash flow at Date 2 so that they earn the required 10 percent return on their Date 1 investment.1\n\nFootnote 1: The same results would occur after an issue of bonds, though the arguments would be less easily presented.\n\nWhat is the value of the firm with this new dividend policy? The new stockholders invest $1,000. They require a 10 percent return, so they will demand $1,000 x 1.10 = $1,100 of the Date 2 cash flow, leaving only $8,900 to the old stockholders. The dividends to the old stockholders will be as follows:\n\n\\[P_{0} = \\frac{\\text{$\\$110}}{1.10} + \\frac{89}{1.10^{2}} \\geq \\$173.55\\]\n\nThis is the same value we had before.\n\n The value of the stock is not affected by this switch in dividend policy even though we have to sell some new stock just to finance the new dividend. In fact, no matter what pattern of dividend payout the firm chooses, the value of the stock will always be the same in this example. In other words, for the Wharton Corporation, dividend policy makes no difference. The reason is simple: Any increase in a dividend at some point in time is exactly offset by a decrease somewhere else; so the net effect, once we account for time value, is zero.
\begin{table}[h!]
    \centering
    \begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|}
        \hline
        \rowcolor{gold} 
        & \textbf{Date 1} & \textbf{Date 2} \\ \hline
        Aggregate dividends to old stockholders & \$11,000 & \$8,900 \\ \hline
        Dividends per share & 110 & 89 \\ \hline
    \end{tabular}
    \caption{Dividends Information}
\end{table}

