Finance term: Required reserves,Basic Banking
Definition: We can examine how a bank rearranges its balance sheet to make a profit when it experiences a change in its deposits. Let's return to the situation when the First National Bank has just received the extra $100 of checkable deposits. As you know, the bank is obliged to keep a certain fraction of its checkable deposits as required reserves. If the fraction (the required reserve ratio) is 10%, the First National Bank's required reserves have increased by $10, and we can rewrite its T-account as follows:
\begin{table}[]
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\multicolumn{4}{l}{First National Bank}                      \\
\multicolumn{2}{l}{Assets} & \multicolumn{2}{l}{Liabilities} \\
Required reserves     & +\$10    & Checkable deposits   & +\$100 \\
Excess reserves		& +\$90	& 	&	 
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Let us assume that the bank chooses not to hold any excess reserves but to make loans instead. The T-account then looks like this:
\begin{table}[]
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\multicolumn{2}{l}{Assets} & \multicolumn{2}{l}{Liabilities} \\
Required reserves     & +\$10    & Checkable deposits   & +\$100 \\
Loans	& +\$90	& 	&	 
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This bank is now making a profit because it holds short-term liabilities such as checkable deposits and uses the proceeds to fund longer-term assets such as loans with higher interest rates. As mentioned earlier, this process of asset transformation is frequently described by saying that banks are in the business of \"borrowing short and lending long,\" For example, if the loans have an interest rate of 10% per year, the bank earns $9 in income from its loans over the year. If the $100 of checkable deposits is in a NOW account with a 5% interest rate and it costs another $3 per year to service the account, the cost per year of these deposits is $8. The banks profit on the new deposits is then $1 per year, plus any interest that is paid on required reserves.

